Students should learn to use AI as a professional mentor and productivity partner—not simply as a tool for copy-pasting answers. AI can help students build real applications professionally, maintain proper documentation throughout a project, learn concepts, break down complex tasks, ask better questions, and continuously improve their work.

The mindset should be: **“How can this tool create value in my life, save my time, improve my skills, and help me create something meaningful?”**

Instead of using AI to avoid thinking, students should use it to **think deeper, explore beyond boundaries, learn faster, document better, and turn ideas into practical solutions.** The real power of AI is not in getting answers—it is in learning how to work with it intelligently to become more capable.







One of the most meaningful things I’ve helped make happen on my campus is through ACE, the Association of Computer Science Engineers.

ACE is a student association where we organize and conduct technical events as part of DASKH, our university’s annual national-level techno-management festival. I currently serve as the Project Lead, working with a team of around 200 technical and non-technical student members.

Being in this role has taught me much more than just organizing events. I’ve learned how to coordinate people, delegate responsibilities, handle challenges, and make sure an idea actually becomes a successful event.

I’ve also had the opportunity to deliver lectures and sessions on cybersecurity, where I could share what I’ve learned with other students.

For me, the biggest achievement is not just conducting an event—it’s bringing people together, creating opportunities for students to learn, and learning how to lead a team along the way.
